<Docs title="How it works" description="CellB's core, its ports, ownership and durability.">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>
    CellB has one runtime core that does not know where it runs, plus an adapter for each
    platform. The same core runs a cell in a tab, in a Node process, or as one node of a celld
    fleet.
  </p>

  <h2>One core, ports for what differs</h2>
  <p>
    The core reaches the platform only through a small set of ports. Each platform supplies its own
    implementation:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>What it is</th><th>Implementations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>OpenCellSql</code></td><td>A synchronous SQLite database per cell</td><td>node:sqlite, SQLite WASM on OPFS, SQLite WASM in memory</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CellStorage</code></td><td>Durability, backup, ownership and R2 objects</td><td><code>celldBucket</code>, <code>filesApiLtxLog</code>, <code>filesApiChangeLog</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Lease</code></td><td>Per-cell ownership with an epoch and a "lost" signal</td><td><code>celldBucket(…).ownership</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ObjectStore</code></td><td>A bucket with conditional writes</td><td>S3 (<code>s3ObjectStore</code>), celld dev's SQLite store, memory, OPFS, IndexedDB (<code>idbObjectStore</code>)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The SQL port is synchronous on purpose. <code>ctx.storage.sql.exec()</code> returns rows at
    once, as on Cloudflare, and both drivers are synchronous underneath.
  </p>

  <h2>The path of a request</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="txt"
    code={`Request ─► runtime.fetch ─► Worker's fetch(req, env, ctx)
                                 │ env.COUNTER.getByName("a")
                                 ▼
                          stub ─┬─ local: the object's host ─► instance
                                ├─ another shard (HTTP over a MessagePort)
                                └─ the owner node (cells-http or celld's tunnel)`}
  />

  <h2>Inside a cell</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Implicit transaction.</strong> The first SQL statement opens a transaction, and it commits when the current turn of the event loop ends.</li>
    <li><strong>Input gate.</strong> Events are queued, so a read-modify-write across storage awaits is safe.</li>
    <li><strong>Output gate.</strong> A reply leaves only after the commit it depends on is durable.</li>
    <li><strong>Reset.</strong> If a commit fails or the lease is lost, the object breaks, and the next call builds a fresh instance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ownership depends on the deployment</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Deployment</th><th>Unit owned</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>One process</td><td>nothing to share</td><td>none needed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A browser origin, sharded</td><td>a shard</td><td>one Web Lock per shard, held by one shard worker</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A fleet bucket (celld, CellB, browsers)</td><td>a cell</td><td>celld's <code>own.json</code> per cell, a node lease per node, epochs and self-fencing</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Durability</h2>
  <p>
    With an LTX log (<code>celldBucket</code> or <code>filesApiLtxLog</code>) every acknowledged
    write is restorable: the RPO is 0. The first commit of a generation is a snapshot, and later
    commits carry only the changed pages. In a browser, SQLite WASM reports those pages through a
    hook on its database file. A periodic backup alone is cheaper, but it can lose up to one
    interval of writes.
  </p>

  <h2>Cells on LTX files in a browser</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>{"bucket: { ltx, node }"}</code>, a browser shard keeps each cell exactly as celld
    keeps it: a snapshot and one LTX file per commit, in an OPFS directory. SQLite never holds the
    database. It reads pages through a VFS from a store worker, with an LRU page cache and
    read-ahead, so a cell's memory is its page cache, whatever its size. Each commit becomes one
    LTX file, written under a temporary name and moved into place. The store compacts a cell into a
    new snapshot when its files grow, and holds a Web Lock per cell, so only one tab or worker
    writes a cell at a time.
  </p>

  <h2>Loaded Workers get a realm of their own</h2>
  <p>
    In Node, a Worker Loader binding always runs each loaded Worker in an isolate of its own: a
    worker thread. In a browser it does so when the host gives an <code>isolateScript</code>, which
    the browser's <code>loadProject</code> sets by default: each loaded Worker then gets a
    Dedicated Worker. Without one (<code>browserProjectRuntime</code> and the shard functions
    unless you pass it), loaded code runs in the realm of the project's worker. In an isolate,
    plain values in its <code>env</code> are copied, and bindings become stubs back to the parent.
    This is how a host runs plugin or agent-written code apart from its own globals; the
    <a href={docHref("packages/cellb/docs/services/dynamic-workers.md")}>Dynamic Workers</a> page
    has the details.
  </p>

